Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;

/*  ⚑ walkRefT -- THE WALK-WRITER ROW, AND A TRIPWIRE FOR THE FLIP LANDING.
    Built C-160 stroke 2, 2026-09-04; R2 RETIRED on its evidence, no edit made.

    A bare accessor DIRECTLY inside a ++ walk, no intervening call, so nothing
    but the walk writer can have aimed lastREF at the read. addrOf beside it, so
    the claim is identity and not merely a string.

    WHAT IT PINS, both arms, measured before any edit:

      arm BARE : aa   bb   wrTarget      <- the fleet pins THIS
      arm FLIP : aa   bb   wrHeld        <- what the same run gives flipped
                 addrOf(wrCur) = one stable node, #1/#2, on both

    ⚠⚠ ROWS 1 AND 2 ARE THE INVARIANT. ROW 3 IS THE TRIPWIRE, AND IT IS PINNED
    TO THE ARM THE FLEET RUNS ON, NOT TO THE ANSWER THAT WILL SURVIVE.
    wrHeld is a member that is ITSELF a holder. Bare reads `wrTarget` because the
    bare road auto-unwraps; the flip reads `wrHeld`, the members own identity,
    preserved. WHEN THE FLIP LANDS THIS ROW GOES RED AND THAT IS THE ROW DOING
    ITS JOB -- the expected value becomes `wrHeld`, and the re-pin is the signal
    that the auto-unwrap is gone. Re-pin it then WITH A SENTENCE, per H6, and do
    not re-pin it before.

    WHY R2 RETIRED, so nobody re-derives it: the walk writers store the HELD --
    the member -- at all four sites, measured, which is what C-158s recon said.
    R2 would have followed one holder level at the write, making the FLIP behave
    like BARE on row 3 and re-introducing for lastREF exactly the auto-unwrap the
    flip exists to remove. `*cursor` is the spelling that reaches a holder
    members target; the writer is not the place for it.  */

register(WalkRefT);
define
    wrBag aa bb;
    wrTarget="TGT";
    wrHeld;
    wrRun code={
        wrHeld := wrTarget;
        wrBag +% wrHeld;
        iterate wrCur attributes on wrBag;
        while ++wrCur;
            cerr "W bare taG =" taG:;
            addrOf(wrCur);
        };
    ;
wrRun();
print "WALKREFT SENTINEL":;
stop();
